The right pattern is to promote **just before** the animation and release **just after** — not to leave the hint on permanently.

```css
.drawer {
  position: fixed;
  inset: 0 auto 0 0;
  width: 320px;
  transform: translateX(-100%);
  transition: transform 250ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1);
}

/* Only while the interaction is live or imminent */
.drawer.is-animating,
.drawer-trigger:hover ~ .drawer {
  will-change: transform;
}

.drawer.is-open { transform: translateX(0); }
```

```js
const drawer = document.querySelector('.drawer');

function openDrawer() {
  drawer.classList.add('is-animating');   // promote
  requestAnimationFrame(() => drawer.classList.add('is-open'));
}

drawer.addEventListener('transitionend', () => {
  drawer.classList.remove('is-animating'); // release the layer
});
```

Two details that make this work. The `rAF` before adding `is-open` gives the browser a frame to create and rasterise the layer, so the first animation frame isn't the one paying for rasterisation. And removing the hint on `transitionend` returns roughly 3-12MB of GPU memory rather than holding it for the rest of the session.

Applying `will-change` on hover of the trigger is the classic refinement: the promotion happens during the ~100ms before the user actually clicks.
